<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Dancing Rabbit Creek</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1830">1830</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Dancing Rabbit <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscogee">Creek</a> was signed on 27 September 1830 in Noxubee County, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a>, the first removal treaty concluded under the Indian Removal Act. The Choctaw ceded about 11 million acres of their remaining homeland in exchange for land in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-territory">Indian Territory</a>. Most were removed west in hard journeys between 1831 and 1833, while some stayed in Mississippi under a clause offering individual land allotments.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-dancing-rabbit-creek.jpg" alt="Treaty of Dancing Rabbit Creek" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rob · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dancing-Rabbit-Creek.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The Choctaw chiefs were to have met President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrew-jackson">Andrew Jackson</a> at Franklin, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tennessee">Tennessee</a>, on 25 August 1830, but Greenwood LeFlore told Secretary of War John H. Eaton that they were fiercely opposed to attending. Jackson instead sent Eaton and General John Coffee as commissioners to meet them in their own country. On 15 September 1830 the commissioners met the chiefs and headmen at Dancing Rabbit Creek, known to the Choctaw as Bok Chukfi Ahilha, and explained the policy of removal through interpreters to some 6,000 people. The Choctaw faced a choice between moving west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a> and living under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and state law as citizens.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>In exchange for their remaining homeland the Choctaw were to receive about 15 million acres in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-territory">Indian Territory</a>, in what became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oklahoma">Oklahoma</a>, with removal to run from 1831 to 1833. The Choctaw Nation's autonomy there was to be secured forever from the laws of states and territories, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> would provide wagons, steamboats and food during removal and for twelve months after. Article 14 allowed a Choctaw head of family who wished to stay and become a citizen to claim 640 acres, with more for children. The chief negotiators, LeFlore, Mosholatubbee and Nittucachee, received land and annuities, and the treaty promised a Choctaw delegate in the House of Representatives. The Senate ratified it in February 1831.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>It was the last major land cession signed by the Choctaw and one of the largest land transfers made between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and American Indians in peacetime. Nearly 15,000 Choctaws, with 1,000 slaves, moved west in stages, and about 2,500 died along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trail-of-tears">Trail of Tears</a>, through journeys that met a cholera epidemic in 1832. The chief George W. Harkins wrote in a farewell letter that the Choctaw chose to suffer and be free rather than live under laws in whose making they had no voice.</p><p>Between 5,000 and 6,000 Choctaws remained in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a> in 1831 and faced years of harassment; in 1849 they described homes burned, fences destroyed and people beaten. The promised delegate was never seated. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oklahoma">Oklahoma</a> the Choctaw Nation's government was later dismantled under the Curtis Act and its lands allotted under the Dawes Act, and those in Mississippi reorganised in 1945 as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-band-of-choctaw-indians">Mississippi Band of Choctaw Indians</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of Dancing Rabbit Creek” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By the <b>Treaty of Dancing Rabbit Creek</b> in 1830 the Choctaw ceded their homeland and were removed to Indian Territory in 1831 to 1833.</blockquote><a href="/state/choctaw-nation">Choctaw Nation</a></li></ul></section>
